.TH WINCYGTERM 1 "November 2026" "wincygterm 1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wincygterm \- run Cygwin programs on a real pty inside Windows Terminal

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B python wincygterm
[\-\-root DIR] [\-\-python EXE] [\-\-term TERM] [\-\-login\-dir] [\-\-no\-reset] [\-\-no\-direct] [\-\-debug FILE]
[\-\-] [\fICMD\fP [\fIARG\fP...]]
.br
.B wincygterm \-\-direct
.I PROG
[\fIARG\fP...]
.br
.B wcdirect
.I PROG
[\fIARG\fP...]
.br
.B python wincygterm
\-\-profile
.br
.B python wincygterm
\-\-version

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wincygterm
runs \fICMD\fP (default \fB/bin/bash \-l\fP) on a real Cygwin pty (\fI/dev/pty*\fP) inside the console it is
started in, typically a Windows Terminal tab. It must be run with a
.B native Windows
Python, not Cygwin's.

A Cygwin program started directly in a Windows Terminal tab talks to Cygwin's own console layer
(\fI/dev/cons*\fP). That layer swallows OSC strings on their way out, so a program cannot ask the terminal for its
colours (OSC 10 and 11), and, behind ssh, key input reaches ncurses programs in fragments, so that a lone ESC is
mistaken for the Escape key and the rest of the sequence is typed as text. A program on a pty, as it is under mintty
or in an ssh session, has neither problem.

You do not need \fBwincygterm\fP in mintty, or in any other terminal that already starts Cygwin programs on a pty:
they are on a pty from the start. It is for Windows Terminal (and other ConPTY-based consoles), where a Cygwin
program is otherwise handed the console layer.

.SS How it works
The file contains two halves. The front-end runs on native Windows Python: it puts its console into raw VT mode,
forwards the bytes typed to the pty, writes what the program prints to the console, and reports window size
changes. Nothing Cygwin sits on the console, so Windows Terminal's answers to queries reach the program. The helper
(started by the front-end with the hidden option \fB\-\-helper\fP, on Cygwin's Python, detached from the console)
starts \fICMD\fP on a Cygwin pty and relays bytes between that pty and the front-end through pipes.

This is the opposite of winpty, which lets a Windows console program run inside a Unix pty.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-root " DIR"
The Cygwin root directory, the one holding \fIbin\\cygwin1.dll\fP. Default: \fB$CYGWIN_ROOT\fP, then the registry,
then \fIC:\\cygwin64\fP, \fIC:\\cygwin\fP, \fID:\\cygwin64\fP, \fID:\\cygwin\fP.
.TP
.BI \-\-python " EXE"
The Cygwin Python (3) to run the pty helper with. Default: the newest \fIpython3.N.exe\fP in \fIDIR\\bin\fP.
.TP
.BI \-\-term " TERM"
\fBTERM\fP for \fICMD\fP. Default: the current \fBTERM\fP, else \fBxterm\-256color\fP. Under Windows Terminal
\fBCOLORTERM=truecolor\fP is also set unless it is already set.
\fBWINCYGTERM\fP is set to the version of \fBwincygterm\fP, which \fBtermprobe\fP(1) reports.
.TP
.B \-\-login\-dir
Start in the home directory. By default \fICMD\fP starts in the current directory (\fBCHERE_INVOKING=1\fP is set
for \fI/etc/profile\fP), which is right for a command, and wrong for a Windows Terminal profile that should land in
the Cygwin home: use this option there.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-reset
When the command ends, \fBwincygterm\fP turns mouse reporting, bracketed paste, kitty keyboard modes and
modifyOtherKeys off, restores attributes and shows the cursor, in case the program died without doing so. This
option skips that.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-direct
Ignore requests from \fBwcdirect\fP (see DIRECT). The secret they need is then not even put in the environment.
.TP
.BI \-\-debug " FILE"
Write the helper's standard error to \fIFILE\fP.
.TP
.B \-\-profile
Print a command line for a Windows Terminal profile and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.

.SH DIRECT
The pty carries text. A Windows console program that wants the console itself, for instance one that asks for mouse
events with \fBENABLE_MOUSE_INPUT\fP (Microsoft Edit does) or draws with \fBWriteConsoleOutput\fP, is started by
Cygwin on a hidden pseudo console, and nothing feeds that console mouse events. It runs, but the mouse is dead.
.PP
Inside the session, \fBwincygterm \-\-direct\fP \fIPROG\fP [\fIARG\fP...] (or \fBwcdirect\fP, a symlink to the same
file) asks the front-end to run \fIPROG\fP on the real console instead. The front-end restores the console modes
it found at start, runs \fIPROG\fP attached to the console, takes the console back and returns \fIPROG\fP's exit
status; the shell sees an ordinary command. \fIPROG\fP is looked up on the Cygwin PATH and passed to Windows as a
Windows path, and it starts in the current directory. The arguments are passed as they are, so give file names the
program can resolve itself (relative names are fine). Use it for native Windows console programs only: a Cygwin
program started this way is back on Cygwin's console layer, and loses what \fBwincygterm\fP is for.
.PP
The shim sends a private OSC string, \fBESC ] 7077 ; direct ; \fP\fITOKEN\fP\fB ; \fP\fIbase64\-JSON\fP\fB BEL\fP, which the
front-end cuts out of the output, and is answered with \fBESC ] 7077 ; ack BEL\fP and, when the program ends,
\fBESC ] 7077 ; exit ; \fP\fISTATUS\fP\fB BEL\fP. \fITOKEN\fP is a random secret created per session and exported to the
shell as \fBWINCYGTERM_TOKEN\fP. Without it a request is dropped silently, so that text from a file you \fBcat\fP, or
from a host you \fBssh\fP to, cannot make your PC run programs. Anything that runs in the session, however, has the
token: this is not a boundary against your own processes. If no answer comes within 3 seconds, \fBwcdirect\fP
fails with status 3.
.PP
Example:
.nf
ln \-s /usr/local/bin/wincygterm ~/bin/wcdirect
wcdirect edit notes.txt
.fi

.SH EXIT STATUS
That of \fICMD\fP (128 plus the signal number if it was killed). 2 if Cygwin, its Python or a console cannot be
found, or if it is run on Cygwin or Linux. \fBwcdirect\fP returns that of \fIPROG\fP (127 if it could not be
started), 2 outside a wincygterm session, 3 if the front-end does not answer.

.SH WINDOWS TERMINAL PROFILE
Add a profile whose command line is the output of \fBpython wincygterm \-\-profile\fP, for instance:
.PP
.nf
"C:\\Python311\\python.exe" "D:\\cygwin\\usr\\local\\bin\\wincygterm" \-\-root "D:\\cygwin" \-\-login\-dir
.fi
.PP
Use the full path of the Windows Python: the path of a profile may differ from that of your shell, and Cygwin's
\fIbin\fP on it could make a bare \fBpython\fP mean the wrong one. After \fBmake install\fP in Cygwin the file
is under the Cygwin root, e.g. \fIusr\\local\\bin\\wincygterm\fP.

.SH TESTED
In Windows Terminal 1.24, with a Cygwin 3.7 development snapshot: an interactive \fBbash\fP, the colour query
(checked with \fBtermprobe\fP), a live \fBtvmail\fP session and a live \fBbidet\fP animation.

The request filter, the shim (on a Cygwin pty, against a stand\-in front\-end) and the request parsing were tested.
The live hand\-over of the console, and Microsoft Edit's mouse through it, have not been tested yet.

.SH LIMITATIONS
Keys reach the program as the plain characters and VT sequences the console produces. A program that asks for
win32\-input\-mode (tvision does) gets plain keys, which it also understands, instead of key records.
Windows programs started from the shell (\fBpwsh\fP, \fBpython.exe\fP) depend on Cygwin's own handling of native
programs on a pty. Start\-up needs two Python processes and takes a moment longer than a plain shell.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR termprobe (1),
.BR tcping (1)
